INT. ABANDONED PLANETARIUM - NIGHT

A great dome of cracked plaster. Rows of tilting seats like rotted teeth. At the center, a Zeiss star projector, shrouded like a sleeping beast.

A flashlight beam wanders across the dome. Behind it: EVELYN HARGROVE (30s, wool coat, camera bag), picking her way down the aisle.

EVELYN
Hello? The caretaker said the doors were unlocked.

Her voice dies in the plaster. She reaches the projector. Lays a hand on its cover.

A SOUND. Soft. Behind her. A seat creaking back.

She spins the light. A MAN sits in the third row. Gray suit. Hands folded. Smiling pleasantly.

THE MAN
Ten minutes early. Punctual people are rare.

EVELYN
I didn't see you come in.

THE MAN
I didn't come in.

She laughs, nervous, and glances at the exit. Still there. Still far.

EVELYN
Are you the caretaker?

THE MAN
I look after the stars. Is that the same?

She lowers the light.

EVELYN
I'm here to photograph the dome. For the historical society.

THE MAN
Of course.

He stands. Slowly. His shadow does not move with him.

EVELYN
(whispering)
Oh.

THE MAN
Don't be frightened. I only want you to look up.

EVELYN
No.

THE MAN
Please.

The projector SHUDDERS under its cover. A hum rises from the machine. A thin line of light cuts from under the cloth.

EVELYN
Who are you?

THE MAN
Ambrose Lyle. Chief astronomer. Nineteen thirty-one.

He smiles, and the plaster above them goes black, then bursts with ten thousand stars.

EVELYN
Please.

AMBROSE
Look up, Miss Hargrove.

She doesn't want to. She does. The stars begin to move toward her.
